      "problem": "$\\frac{11}{(\\frac{3}{4})}=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, remember that a fraction represents a division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{11}{(\\frac{3}{4})}=11÷\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $11=\\frac{11}{1}$ and dividing by $\\frac{3}{4}$ is the same as multiplying by its reciprocal $\\frac{4}{3}.$ We can use these facts to simplify the fraction above: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11÷\\frac{3}{4} & = \\\\ \\frac{11}{1}\\times \\frac{4}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{11\\times 4}{1\\times 3} & = \\\\ \\frac{44}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{11}{(\\frac{3}{4})}=\\frac{44}{3}.\n$$",
